learning.detailAuthoring.setComponent("main",html`<main class="page"><h2>The pretraining learning loop</h2><p class="lead">A randomly initialized model learns to assign probability to the next token, given the preceding tokens. Continued pretraining can instead start from existing weights.</p><ol><li><strong>Forward pass.</strong> Token embeddings and position information enter repeated transformer blocks. Causal self-attention mixes information from allowed earlier positions; feed-forward layers transform each position. Residual connections and normalization support stable training.</li><li><strong>Predict.</strong> A vocabulary projection produces logits. Softmax turns them into a distribution over the next token. During training, the known preceding tokens are supplied rather than sampled model predictions.</li><li><strong>Measure error.</strong> Cross-entropy penalizes low probability on the actual next token. Causal masking allows prediction losses for many positions in a sequence to be computed in parallel.</li><li><strong>Update.</strong> Backpropagation computes gradients; an optimizer such as AdamW updates weights. Learning-rate schedules, clipping and numerical precision affect stability.</li><li><strong>Repeat and checkpoint.</strong> Train over many batches, validate on held-out text and save recoverable checkpoints. At large scale, distribute data and model computation across accelerators.</li></ol><p class="equation">L = −(1/N) Σᵢ log pθ(tᵢ | t₁, …, tᵢ₋₁)<br>θ = model weights; N = number of scored target tokens</p><p class="note">Lower held-out loss indicates better predictive fit to that data; it does not by itself establish truthfulness, instruction following or safe behavior. Model size, context length, token count and compute budget are coupled design choices.</p></main>`,styles);
post.detailAuthoring.setComponent("main",html`<main class="page"><h2>Adapt a base model into an assistant</h2><p class="lead">Pretraining produces a text continuation model. Post-training can make its responses more useful for a particular interaction or task.</p><ul><li><strong>Supervised fine-tuning (SFT).</strong> Train on prompt–response demonstrations using token prediction loss, commonly scoring only the desired response tokens.</li><li><strong>Preference optimization.</strong> Use comparisons of candidate answers. RLHF can train a reward model and optimize a response policy; DPO directly trains on preferred versus rejected responses without a separate reinforcement-learning rollout loop.</li><li><strong>Task and safety adaptation.</strong> Include relevant task examples, tool-use demonstrations and behavior constraints; test for regressions as well as improvements.</li></ul><p class="note">These are optional recipes, not one universal sequence. Full fine-tuning or parameter-efficient adapters can be used. Preference labels encode judgments and can contain bias; better style can conceal persistent factual errors.</p></main>`,styles);
evaluate.detailAuthoring.setComponent("main",html`<main class="page"><h2>Decide whether the model is ready</h2><p class="lead">Use several kinds of evidence. No single score captures the quality or risk of a deployed assistant.</p><ul><li><strong>Generalization.</strong> Track held-out cross-entropy and, where useful, perplexity. Check duplication and benchmark contamination; compare metrics only with compatible tokenization and evaluation setups.</li><li><strong>Usefulness.</strong> Evaluate representative tasks, instruction following, factuality and human judgments, including difficult or uncommon cases.</li><li><strong>Risk and robustness.</strong> Probe harmful outputs, privacy leakage, prompt injection where tools or retrieved content are used, and performance across relevant user groups and languages.</li><li><strong>Serving feasibility.</strong> Measure latency, throughput, memory and cost under realistic request lengths and concurrency.</li></ul><p class="note">Set release criteria for the intended use. Validation guides development; a held-out final test limits tuning to the test set. Failed checks may require new data, training changes, product constraints or a delayed release.</p></main>`,styles);
serve.detailAuthoring.setComponent("main",html`<main class="page"><h2>Serve the model and learn from operation</h2><p class="lead">Deployment combines a trained model with a reliable inference system and a controlled release process.</p><ol><li><strong>Package a reproducible artifact.</strong> Version weights, tokenizer, chat template, configuration and the evaluation report together.</li><li><strong>Optimize inference.</strong> Batch requests, cache attention keys and values, and consider quantization or distillation. Re-evaluate the actual serving artifact: compression can change quality.</li><li><strong>Generate responses.</strong> Run a prompt through the model, select a next token using a decoding policy, append it and repeat until a stop condition. Ordinary inference does not update model weights.</li><li><strong>Release and observe.</strong> Roll out gradually with rollback capability. Monitor latency, failures, cost, task quality and misuse signals while handling collected feedback appropriately.</li><li><strong>Improve deliberately.</strong> Investigate drift and failures. Curate feedback into future datasets, retrain or post-train, then repeat evaluation before updating the deployed version.</li></ol><p class="note">Retrieval, system prompts and tools can change behavior without changing weights. Monitoring alone does not repair a model, and live conversations do not automatically become training updates.</p></main>`,styles);
